Band power of the descriptors leaves no gap between bands: a band holds the PSD bins in `[low, high)` and its power is their sum times the frequency step, and the total behind relative power is the same sum over `[fmin, fmax)`, so the relative powers of bands that tile the window sum to 1. It was a trapezoid over the bins inside each band, which left out the strip between adjacent bands: the canonical bands summed to about 0.93 and white-noise delta power was 79 % of its true value. Corrected band power and the assignment of fitted peaks to bands, `alpha_peak_freq` included, use the same half-open rule.

The Welch PSD of the descriptors uses 2 s segments with 50 % overlap, set by `welch_segment_length` and `welch_overlap` on `BandDescriptorConfig` and `ParametricDescriptorConfig`. It used at most 256 samples per segment, whatever the sampling rate: 0.78 Hz resolution at 200 Hz, and the last 208 samples of a 10 s epoch were dropped. Band powers, relative powers, ratios and parametric fits computed with Welch change and must be recomputed.

`compute_row_outlier_scores`, and with it `drop_epoch_outliers` and the subject outlier helpers, no longer scores a feature whose MAD is zero, and takes each row's outlier fraction over the finite, scored values. A zero MAD fell back to the mean absolute deviation, which flagged any change in a nearly constant feature (a peak count of 2 among counts of 1) as an extreme outlier, and missing values counted in the denominator, so an epoch needed more than the threshold of its finite features to be dropped.

The complexity descriptors are computed on each epoch centred on its own mean, and `ComplexityDescriptorConfig.bandpass = (l_freq, h_freq)` band-passes each epoch first with `mne.filter.filter_data`. Zero crossings and RMS were taken around zero, so an offset or slow drift changed them.

Log band power of a band with no power (a flat channel) is `NaN`, recorded as a failure, instead of `log10(eps) = -15.65`, a finite value that later steps took for data.

`ParametricDescriptorConfig.min_peak_height` sets specparam's minimum peak height above the aperiodic fit (log10 power); it was not exposed, so every fit used 0.

A descriptor table is one self-describing parquet file: `save_descriptor_table` keeps the feature-column list in `df.attrs["feature_columns"]`, which pandas stores in the parquet metadata, instead of a `_feature_columns.json` sidecar. `load_descriptor_table`, `merge_descriptor_tables` and `check_feature_column_consistency` read the list from the table (new `feature_columns_of`); their sidecar arguments and the CSV output of `save_descriptor_table` are removed.

`generate_descriptor_subject_report` builds one report for several conditions: `overview_df` has one row per condition, named together in the title, a `condition` column in the tables becomes one of their selectors, and every entry of `figure_paths` is shown in its order, its key the caption. The title named the condition of the first row only, and only five fixed figure keys were shown.

`compute_constant_feature_summary`, and with it the descriptor QC and `select_viable_feature_columns`, marks a column constant when its standard deviation is at most `tol` times its mean absolute value (new `relative_std` column), not when it is at most `tol`. Absolute band power in V² varies by less than 1e-12 V² between epochs and was marked constant.

`aggregate_family_qc`'s `failure_rate` is the share of a family's (channel, observation) slots with a failure, counting only the failures of the observations in the table (by `id_col`). It divided the number of failure records, from several channels and from rejected epochs, by the number of observations, so one channel failing in every epoch read as 100 % and could exceed 1.
